  visit_site: "Visit site", back_top: "← Back to home",
  gv_title: "Getting Started — A Learning Guide to Aromatherapy & Self-Care",
  gv_intro: "This page is a roadmap for people about to learn aromatherapy and self-care. Especially if you do not know where to start, deciding on an order is the shortcut.",
  gv_flow_h: "The overall flow: four steps",
  gv_s1: "<strong>Step 1, “Aromatherapy Basics,”</strong> covers what essential oils are, which parts of the plant they come from and how (steam distillation, cold pressing and so on), how to read the Latin and family names on labels, how to judge quality, how to store oils, and safety principles. Skipping this step makes later practice harder. Start with the introductory article “What Are Essential Oils?”.",
  gv_s2: "<strong>Step 2, “Essential Oil Profiles,”</strong> studies oils one bottle at a time. The key is to learn components, scent, traditionally used situations and cautions as a set. Start with one familiar oil such as lavender. This site does not state effects as certainties; it introduces traditional uses as such.",
  gv_s3: "<strong>Step 3, “Self-Care Practice,”</strong> moves what you have learned into daily life: settling the breathing, bathing ideas, self-massage basics, how to rest, and seasonal care. Every practice comes with safety notes and, where needed, guidance on when to seek medical care. Build up small practices you can continue, rather than “effortful care.”",
  gv_s4: "<strong>Step 4, “Blending & Materials,”</strong> builds the skill to assemble scents yourself. The focus is dilution and concentration math, the types and characteristics of carrier oils, and how to balance a scent. Take this step after you understand the basics and safety principles.",
  gv_tips_h: "Tips for learning",
  gv_t1: "Do not try to memorize everything at once — start with one oil, one practice",
  gv_t2: "Impressions of scent change with your condition and the season. Noting the date and your impressions helps learning stick",
  gv_t3: "For practices on skin, always start with dilution and a patch test. Read the safety page first",
